Setting $\alpha$ to be the angle between $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix},$ we have
\[\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} + (\cos \alpha) \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix} + (\sin \alpha) \begin{pmatrix} -x \\ -y \\ -z \end{pmatrix}.\]Then $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{(cos \alpha) - (sin \alpha)} \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}.$  We want the components of $\begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \\ z \end{pmatrix}$ to be integers, so we choose $\alpha$ so that $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ are integers.  For example, we can choose $\alpha = \frac{\pi}{3},$ which gives
\[\mathbf{p} = \begin{pmatrix} 1/2 \\ \sqrt{3}/2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.\]Then the normal vector of plane $P$ is $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ \sqrt{3} \\ 2 \end{pmatrix}}.$ </think> <answer> \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ \sqrt{3} \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} </answer>

